Subject: [PATCH 2/2] claude: Check pkg before calling a package missing
To: Pat Maddox <pat@patmaddox.com>
Cc: lab-devel@p7x.dev
Change-Id: pxwyzuzoxlrtnsqsxzywowqunmvyomky

A failed command or load was taken as proof that a package was not
installed, and reported so on the list. notmuch-emacs was installed;
emacs -Q had only left its site-lisp directory off load-path. The
claim was wrong, the test it excused was skipped, and Pat spent a
round trip finding out.

pkg answers the question directly, so ask it before reporting a
dependency as missing.
---
change-id: pxwyzuzoxlrtnsqsxzywowqunmvyomky

 CLAUDE.md | 5 +++++
 1 file changed, 5 insertions(+)

diff --git a/CLAUDE.md b/CLAUDE.md
index 8d769f1..ecb90f5 100644
--- a/CLAUDE.md
+++ b/CLAUDE.md
@@ -224,3 +224,8 @@ are how each of them earns trust.
 - Do not install dependencies.
 - If you get blocked on a missing dependency, report to user the
   dependencies that need to be installed.
+- Before you call a package missing, ask pkg: `pkg info -x <name>`,
+  or `pkg which <file>` for a command or file. A failed command or
+  load shows only that it failed. The cause may be your invocation, as
+  when `emacs -Q` leaves site-lisp off load-path. Report what pkg says,
+  not what you inferred.
